    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/piedmontu/jobs/5547449/counselor-behavioral-health-athens-and-online-students": {
      "title": "Counselor, Behavioral Health, Athens and Online Students",
      "description": "Counselor, Behavioral Health, Athens and Online Students Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Athens Campus Job Type Part-Time Staff Job Number 202600182 Division Student Affairs Department Counseling and Career Services Opening Date 11/11/2026 Description Benefits Founded in 1897, Piedmont University is a private, comprehensive liberal arts university with an enrollment of approximately 2,000 students at campuses in Demorest and Athens, Georgia. Description The Behavioral Health Counselor – Athens & Online Services expands access to behavioral health services for students attending Piedmont University's Athens campus and students enrolled in the University's online academic programs. Working as part of the Piedmont HOPE initiative, the Counselor provides individual counseling, crisis intervention, suicide prevention, behavioral health screening, referral coordination, telebehavioral health services, consultation, outreach, and case management to increase timely access to care for students who may otherwise experience geographic, scheduling, or logistical barriers to receiving services. The Counselor serves as the primary behavioral health provider for Athens-based students while coordinating behavioral health services for online students across the University. The position also plays a key role in implementing HOPE Check, expanding suicide prevention efforts, strengthening referral networks, and increasing student engagement with behavioral health resources. Essential Functions and Responsibilities Clinical Counseling Services Provide confidential short-term individual counseling to currently enrolled Piedmont University students. Conduct biopsychosocial assessments, treatment planning, crisis intervention, suicide risk assessments, and safety planning. Utilize evidence-based, trauma-informed, culturally responsive counseling interventions appropriate for college students. Provide walk-in, urgent, and scheduled counseling appointments. Maintain timely and accurate clinical documentation in the University's electronic health record. Evaluate treatment progress and modify interventions as clinically indicated. Coordinate referrals for students requiring psychiatric care, long-term therapy, substance use treatment, or specialized behavioral health services. Provide consultation to faculty, staff, parents (when appropriate), and University departments regarding student behavioral health concerns. Athens Campus Services Serve as the primary Counseling Services clinician assigned to the Athens campus by: Maintaining regular office hours on the Athens campus. Providing individual counseling and crisis intervention. Participating in orientation and welcome events. Developing outreach activities specifically for Athens students. Providing consultation to Athens faculty and staff. Participating in CARE Team activities as requested. Increasing visibility and awareness of Counseling Services among graduate, commuter, hybrid, and adult learners. Online Student Behavioral Health Services Serve as the primary behavioral health clinician for Piedmont University's online student population. Responsibilities include: Coordinating behavioral health services for online students. Maintaining regular communication with online students receiving counseling services. Providing secure telebehavioral health counseling services in accordance with Georgia licensure laws and University policy. Conducting suicide risk assessments, crisis intervention, and safety planning for online students. Coordinating referrals to behavioral health providers in students' local communities when long-term or specialized treatment is needed. Developing individualized follow-up plans to improve treatment engagement and continuity of care. Collaborating with Online Learning, Academic Advising, Student Success, and other departments to identify students in need of behavioral health support. Developing outreach initiatives specifically designed to increase behavioral health awareness among online learners. Monitoring utilization trends and recommending improvements in services for online students. HOPE Check Coordination Serve as the clinical lead for HOPE Check implementation for Athens and online students. Responsibilities include: Coordinating distribution of behavioral health screenings. Reviewing screening results. Identifying students requiring clinical follow-up. Contacting students identified through screening. Conducting additional risk assessments when appropriate. Completing safety planning and crisis intervention when indicated. Referring students to Counseling Services or community providers. Monitoring completion rates and participation. Collaborating with the Behavioral Health Services Coordinator on implementation logistics. Assisting with continuous improvement of the screening process. Outreach, Prevention, and Education Develop and present behavioral health programming for Athens and online students. Participate in Every Lion Counts, Wellness Wednesdays, and other Piedmont HOPE events. Deliver QPR and other gatekeeper trainings as assigned. Promote help-seeking behaviors, resilience, recovery, and stigma reduction. Collaborate with HOPE Guides during outreach activities. Develop online educational content promoting behavioral health resources. Community Partnerships and Referral Coordination Maintain working relationships with behavioral health providers in Athens and surrounding communities. Coordinate referrals with hospitals, crisis providers, substance use treatment providers, and community mental health agencies. Participate in the Piedmont HOPE Learning Collaborative. Assist with strengthening referral pathways and continuity of care between campus and community providers. Program Evaluation and Grant Support Collect and maintain clinical and program data required by Piedmont HOPE. Track: Athens utilization Online utilization HOPE Check follow-up Crisis interventions Referrals Outreach participation Student outcomes Assist with annual reports, needs assessments, and grant reporting. Participate in program evaluation and continuous quality improvement initiatives. Additional Responsibilities Participate in staff meetings, clinical consultation, and professional development. Maintain licensure and continuing education requirements. Follow University policies and ethical standards governing professional counseling. Other relevant duties as assigned. Regular and predictable attendance is required. On-site work is required. Incumbents must remain relevant in the area of employment and flexible to changes in work related to institutional needs. Typical Qualifications Education and Experience Master's Degree in Counseling, Social Work, or Marriage and Family Therapy from a regionally accredited college or university. Demonstrated passion for working with students and experience working in a team-oriented environment on a college campus considered an asset. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education counseling. Experience providing tele-behavioral health services. Experience with suicide prevention and crisis intervention. Experience implementing behavioral health screening programs. Experience working with online or non-traditional students. Experience conducting outreach and psychoeducational programming. Knowledge of Athens-area behavioral health resources. Licenses and Certification Current independent Georgia licensure as one of the following: Licensed Professional Counselor (LPC) or associate (LAPC) Licensed Clinical Social Worker (LCSW) or associate (LMSW) Licensed Marriage and Family Therapist (LMFT) or associate (LAMFT) Valid Georgia Driver’s License (or obtained within 30 days of hire date) and satisfactory MVR. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of college student development and behavioral health. Knowledge of evidence-based counseling interventions. Knowledge of suicide assessment, crisis intervention, and safety planning. Knowledge of co-occurring mental health and substance use disorders. Ability to provide culturally responsive, trauma-informed care. Knowledge of HIPAA, FERPA, ADA, Title IX, and ethical standards governing clinical practice. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Ability to build collaborative relationships across multiple campuses and departments. Ability to work independently while maintaining close collaboration with the Counseling Services team. Ability to effectively balance clinical services, outreach, consultation, screening coordination, and program development within a part-time schedule. Supplemental Information Environmental, Physical, and Visual Acuity Factors Work will be performed in indoor environmental conditions: Protection from weather conditions but not necessarily from temperature changes. Incumbents will perform work that is mostly sedentary, where they exert up to 10 pounds of force occasionally and/or a negligible amount of force frequently or constantly to lift, carry, push, pull, or otherwise move objects, including the human body. Incumbents will regularly get up and down from a seated position, reach, bend, and stand throughout the day. Incumbents are required to have close visual acuity to perform an activity such as preparing and analyzing data and figures, transcribing, viewing a computer terminal, and extensive reading. Non-Discrimination Policy Piedmont University has a strong institutional commitment to the achievement of diversity within its faculty, staff, and students. It is the policy of Piedmont University not to discriminate in its educational programs, activities, or employment on the basis of sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, physical or mental disability, pregnancy, race, age, religion, color, national or ethnic origin, veteran status, genetic information, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement It is the policy of Piedmont University not to discriminate in its educational programs, activities, or employment on the basis of sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, physical or mental disability, pregnancy, race, age, religion, color, national or ethnic origin, veteran status, genetic information, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Inquiries or complaints from students or employees concerning the prohibition of discrimination on the basis of sex in this statement should be directed to: Coordinator for Title IX, Mia Borgstrom Daniel Hall 213 Demorest, GA 30535 (706) 778-8500 x1504 titleix@piedmont.edu Inquiries or complaints from students, other than on the basis of sex, concerning any of the prohibitions of discrimination in the statement should be directed to: 504 Coordinator, Letitia Roller Daniel Hall, – Room 208 1021 Central Avenue P.O. BOX 10 Demorest, GA 30535 (706) 778-8500 oars@piedmont.edu Inquiries or complaints from employees concerning any of the prohibitions of discrimination in this statement should be directed to: Letitia Roller Assistant Vice President of Human Resources and Compliance Daniel Hall – Room 208 1021 Central Avenue P.O. BOX 10 Demorest, GA 30535 (706) 778-8500 x 2858 hr@piedmont.edu We offer a benefits package to full-time 9-month and 10-month employees, including: Health Insurance Vision Insurance Dental Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts TIAA 403(b) Retirement - with up to 4% employer matching Life Insurance Long-Term Disability Insurance Holidays Educational Assistance Program (Employees and their dependents) On-campus Fitness Center and access to Habersham County's Ruby Fulbright Aquatic Center Employer Piedmont University Address 1021 Central Avenue P.O Box 10 Demorest, Georgia, 30535 Phone 706-778-3000 Website http://www.piedmont.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/piedmontu/jobs/5547452/coordinator-behavioral-health-services": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Behavioral Health Services",
      "description": "Coordinator, Behavioral Health Services Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Demorest (Main) Campus Job Type Full-Time Staff Job Number 202600183 Division Student Affairs Department Counseling and Career Services Opening Date 11/11/2026 Description Benefits Founded in 1897, Piedmont University is a private, comprehensive liberal arts university with an enrollment of approximately 2,000 students at campuses in Demorest and Athens, Georgia. Description The Coordinator of Behavioral Health Services serves as the primary administrative, operational, and student-access point for Counseling Services and Piedmont HOPE. The Coordinator provides a welcoming, confidential, and trauma-informed first point of contact for students seeking behavioral health support while coordinating appointments, intake processes, referrals, outreach activities, community partnerships, program documentation, and data collection. The position supports implementation of HOPE Check, HOPE Guides, the Piedmont HOPE Learning Collaborative, and other suicide-prevention and behavioral-health initiatives. By assuming essential administrative and program-support responsibilities, the Coordinator increases the capacity of licensed clinicians to provide counseling, crisis intervention, consultation, and direct student care. This expanded description builds on the responsibilities in Piedmont’s existing position document while more clearly reflecting the role’s responsibilities within Piedmont HOPE. Essential Functions and Responsibilities Student Access and Administrative Support Maintain a hospitable, welcoming, private, and trauma-informed environment for students and visitors. Serve as the primary telephone, email, and in-person contact for Counseling Services. Respond to routine questions and provide students with clear information about available behavioral health services. Schedule counseling appointments, walk-in visits, follow-up contacts, and staff meetings. Assist students with intake forms, screening procedures, appointment requests, and other access-related processes. Recognize situations requiring immediate clinical attention and promptly connect the student with a licensed counselor or appropriate emergency resource. Provide information about campus and community resources while remaining within the non-clinical scope of the position. Support the operation of the Student Access Center as a confidential and welcoming entry point for behavioral health services. Referral and Care-Coordination Support Maintain current information about campus, community, crisis, substance-use, recovery, and specialized behavioral health resources. Assist students with accessing referrals recommended by Counseling Services clinicians. Support communication with community providers, hospitals, crisis agencies, and other referral partners as authorized and appropriate. Maintain referral directories, resource guides, warm-handoff procedures, and follow-up documentation. Help students understand how to access after-hours services, 988, Campus Safety, emergency services, and other crisis resources. Support coordination of post-crisis and hospital-discharge follow-up under the direction of clinical staff. Piedmont HOPE Program Support Coordinate administrative and logistical activities associated with Piedmont HOPE. Support implementation of HOPE Check, including scheduling screening opportunities, preparing materials, tracking participation, and assisting with non-clinical follow-up procedures. Assist with the recruitment, onboarding, scheduling, training logistics, and documentation of HOPE Guides. Maintain HOPE Guide outreach kits, educational materials, branded supplies, and event resources. Support QPR, Mental Health First Aid, professional-development activities, and other behavioral health training programs. Coordinate registration, facilities, materials, attendance records, certificates, and evaluation forms for trainings. Assist with Every Lion Counts, Wellness Wednesdays, orientation, awareness campaigns, resilience workshops, and other outreach activities. Support outreach and programming for the Athens campus, online students, graduate students, adult learners, and other priority populations. Learning Collaborative and Community Partnerships Provide administrative support for the Piedmont HOPE Learning Collaborative. Coordinate meetings, regional trainings, continuing-education events, tabletop exercises, and community-learning activities. Maintain partner contact lists, meeting schedules, attendance records, correspondence, and partnership documentation. Assist with communications involving behavioral health providers, hospitals, first responders, law enforcement, schools, recovery organizations, and community agencies. Support the Piedmont HOPE Advisory Committee by preparing agendas, meeting materials, minutes, reports, and follow-up communications. Help maintain letters of commitment, memoranda of understanding, referral agreements, and other partnership records. Records, Technology, and Data Support Serve as a content manager or designated administrative user for Counseling Services’ clinical software and electronic health record system. Support appointment scheduling, electronic forms, screening workflows, automated communications, and administrative reporting within the system. Organize and maintain confidential correspondence, files, program records, and administrative documentation. Follow all university requirements related to confidentiality, record security, FERPA, and applicable privacy standards. Enter, maintain, and compile non-clinical program data, including: Students screened Outreach participation Training attendance Referrals Follow-up contacts HOPE Guide activities Community-partner participation Produce monthly, quarterly, and annual statistical reports in collaboration with the Director of Counseling Services and Institutional Effectiveness. Assist with surveys, evaluation forms, needs assessments, annual reports, and grant-required performance measures. Maintain accurate documentation while ensuring that reports use aggregate or de-identified information when appropriate. Financial and Operational Support Place orders and maintain inventories of outreach, training, office, screening, and educational materials. Submit invoices, track expenditures, reconcile purchases, and assist with financial reports. Maintain documentation supporting grant expenditures and university in-kind contributions. Coordinate room reservations, technology, printing, registration, signage, and supplies for behavioral health events. Assist with grant timelines, deliverables, reporting deadlines, and required project documentation. Update departmental handbooks, resource guides, forms, procedures, and program descriptions. Support compliance with university policies and relevant accreditation, licensing, grant, and program requirements. Perform other relevant duties as assigned. Scope and Boundaries of the Position The Coordinator is a non-clinical position and does not provide counseling, conduct clinical assessments, determine suicide risk, diagnose behavioral health conditions, or make independent treatment decisions. Students presenting with urgent, crisis, or clinical concerns will be promptly connected with a licensed clinician, Campus Safety, 988, 911, or another appropriate crisis resource. Additional Requirements Regular and predictable attendance is required. On-site work is required. Occasional travel between Piedmont University locations or to regional partnership events may be required. Some evening or special-event hours may occasionally be necessary. A valid Georgia driver’s license, or the ability to obtain one within 30 days of hire, and a satisfactory motor-vehicle record may be required Typical Qualifications Education and Experience High school diploma or equivalent required. One to five years of administrative, customer-service, student-services, healthcare, behavioral-health, or related experience. Demonstrated experience working with confidential or sensitive information. Sincere interest in supporting college students and improving access to behavioral health services. Preferred Qualifications Experience with electronic health records, scheduling systems, databases, survey tools, or grant reporting. Familiarity with suicide-prevention, crisis-response, substance-use, recovery, or community-referral resources. Licenses and Certifications Valid Georgia Driver’s License (or obtained within 30 days of hire date) and satisfactory MVR. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Excellent verbal, written, organizational, customer-service, and administrative skills. Ability to provide a calm, respectful, inclusive, and trauma-informed response to students who may be distressed. Ability to maintain strict confidentiality and exercise sound judgment with sensitive information. Strong attention to detail and ability to maintain accurate records. Ability to prioritize multiple responsibilities in a fast-paced environment with frequent interruptions. Ability to work independently while collaborating effectively with counselors, university departments, students, and community partners. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build productive relationships across a diverse university community. Proficiency in Microsoft Word, Excel, Outlook, and related office technology. Ability to learn university systems, electronic health record software, scheduling platforms, databases, web-based forms, and reporting tools. Ability to coordinate events, trainings, meetings, outreach activities, and community partnerships. Supplemental Information Environmental, Physical, and Visual Acuity Factors Essential functions and responsibilities of this position are performed in an inside environmental condition, where there is protection from weather conditions but not necessarily temperature changes. Essential functions and responsibilities require light work: Exerting up to 20 pounds of force occasionally, and/or up to 10 pounds of force frequently, and/or a negligible amount of force constantly to move objects. If the use of arm and/or leg controls requires exertion of forces greater than that for Sedentary Work and the worker sits most of the time, the job is rated for Light Work. Essential functions and responsibilities require incumbents to: climb or ascend and descend stairs or ramps using feet and legs; stoop by bending the body downward and forward by bending the spine at the waist; kneel by bending legs to come to rest on knee or knees; crouch by bending the body downward and forward by bending leg and spine; reach or extend hands and arms in any direction; stand by remaining upright on the feet; walk or move about on foot to accomplish tasks; lift by raising objects from a lower to a higher position or moving objects horizontally from position-to-position; finger, grasp, or feel objects; talk to exchange and express ideas; and to hear to perceive the nature of sounds at normal speaking levels. Incumbents must have close visual acuity to perform activities such as extensive reading and operate computers and other office equipment. Non-Discrimination Policy Piedmont University has a strong institutional commitment to the achievement of diversity within its faculty, staff, and students. It is the policy of Piedmont University not to discriminate in its educational programs, activities, or employment on the basis of sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, physical or mental disability, pregnancy, race, age, religion, color, national or ethnic origin, veteran status, genetic information, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement It is the policy of Piedmont University not to discriminate in its educational programs, activities, or employment on the basis of sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, physical or mental disability, pregnancy, race, age, religion, color, national or ethnic origin, veteran status, genetic information, or any other category protected by applicable federal, state, or local law. Inquiries or complaints from students or employees concerning the prohibition of discrimination on the basis of sex in this statement should be directed to: Coordinator for Title IX, Mia Borgstrom Daniel Hall 213 Demorest, GA 30535 (706) 778-8500 x1504 titleix@piedmont.edu Inquiries or complaints from students, other than on the basis of sex, concerning any of the prohibitions of discrimination in the statement should be directed to: 504 Coordinator, Letitia Roller Daniel Hall, – Room 208 1021 Central Avenue P.O. BOX 10 Demorest, GA 30535 (706) 778-8500 oars@piedmont.edu Inquiries or complaints from employees concerning any of the prohibitions of discrimination in this statement should be directed to: Letitia Roller Assistant Vice President of Human Resources and Compliance Daniel Hall – Room 208 1021 Central Avenue P.O. BOX 10 Demorest, GA 30535 (706) 778-8500 x 2858 hr@piedmont.edu We offer a complete benefits package to full-time 12-month employees, including: Health Insurance Vision Insurance Dental Insurance Flexible Spending Accounts TIAA 403(b) Retirement - with up to 4% employer matching Life Insurance Long-Term Disability Insurance Holidays Vacation Medical Leave (Sick Leave) Educational Assistance Program (Employees and their dependents) On-campus Fitness Center and access to Habersham County's Ruby Fulbright Aquatic Center Employer Piedmont University Address 1021 Central Avenue P.O Box 10 Demorest, Georgia, 30535 Phone 706-778-3000 Website http://www.piedmont.edu Apply"
    }
  },
